{
  "summary": "Round 14's critique of round 13 is correct that 'the seams are already there' is an observation, not a design, and that an explicit config field is required. But round 14 also validates the terminal_stage approach without addressing its one genuine implementation subtlety: the spec-writing block (line 1657) has no stage guard and runs unconditionally after the plan review block completes. This means a terminal_phase='plan' configuration cannot simply 'not enter' the spec block — it must explicitly return before line 1657. The cleanest implementation is therefore: (1) add a `terminal_phase` field to board config with values 'plan', 'spec', or null (default = full pipeline); (2) after plan review PASS at lines 1600-1608, if terminal_phase='plan', route to final-human-approval instead of approved and return immediately — this is a 3-line change; (3) after the spec review loop at line 1676, if terminal_phase='spec', route to final-human-approval instead of implementing and return — another 3-line change. The key design decision round 14 got right but didn't emphasize enough: the check must happen after the review passes, not before the block starts, because the review is the quality gate that validates the work product before the human sees it. Routing to final-human-approval after a passing plan review means the human gets a reviewed plan; routing before review means they get an unreviewed one. This also means the config field semantics are 'last phase whose review must pass before human approval', not 'last phase that runs'. The skip-early-phases use case remains out of scope, which is correct — it's a fundamentally different problem requiring data contract enforcement that doesn't exist yet."
}
